    {
      "slug": "holy-trinity-masaccio",
      "title": "Holy Trinity (Masaccio)",
      "year": 1425,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Masaccio%2C_trinit%C3%A0.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 1028,
      "imageHeight": 2073,
      "story": "The Holy Trinity, with the Virgin and Saint John and donors is a fresco by the Italian Renaissance artist Masaccio in the Dominican church of Santa Maria Novella, in Florence. The fresco was among Masaccio's last major commissions and is often cited as one of the first monumental Renaissance paintings to utilize linear perspective.",
      "facts": [
        "Records of the Berti family, discovered in 2012, indicate that they owned a tomb at the foot of the fresco.",
        "Around 1568, Cosimo I, then Duke of Florence, commissioned Giorgio Vasari to undertake extensive renovation work at Santa Maria Novella, including reconfiguring and redecorating the area where Masaccio's fresco was located.",
        "Masaccio's Holy Trinity was rediscovered when Vasari's altar was dismantled during renovations in 1860.",
        "Restoration was done to the Crucifixion section of the painting at that time, to replace missing areas of the design; mostly architectural details around the perimeter of the work."
      ],
